.TH STAT 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
stat \- display the status of files or file systems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stat
.RB [ \-fLt ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR format ]
.IR file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stat
prints the status of each
.IR file ,
or with
.B \-f
of the file system that holds it, on standard output.
This
.B stat
is the busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
At least one
.I file
is required.
The operands are examined in order.
One that cannot be examined gets a message on standard error, and
.B stat
goes on to the next.
.PP
A symbolic link is described as itself, not as its target, unless
.B \-L
says to follow it.
Junctions, volume mount points, and app execution aliases count as
symbolic links.
.PP
The output for each file is a block of lines, or with
.B \-t
a single line, or with
.B \-c
whatever
.I format
says.
It always ends with a newline, with one exception described under
.BR \-c .
.SS Default output
A file gets these lines, in order:
.IP \(bu 2
.B File:
shows the name as given, or for a symbolic link its name, an arrow, and
its target, each in single quotes.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Size:
shows the size in bytes,
.B Blocks:
the number of blocks allocated,
.B "IO Block:"
the I/O block size, and then the type of the file.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Device:
shows the device number in hex followed by an
.BR h ,
a slash, and the same number in decimal followed by a
.BR d ;
.B Inode:
the inode number; and
.B Links:
the number of hard links.
A character device adds
.B Device type:
and its major and minor numbers in hex.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Access:
shows, in parentheses, the permissions in octal as
.B %04a
prints them, a slash, and the string of
.BR %A .
.B Uid:
and
.B Gid:
each show, in parentheses, the id in 5 columns, a slash, and the name in
8 columns.
.IP \(bu 2
.BR Access: ,
.BR Modify: ,
and
.B Change:
show the times of last access, modification, and change, which is the
creation time.
.PP
A time is local time, in the form
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
2026\-10\-19 21:20:34.592248200 \-0400
.EE
.RE
.PP
with the offset from UTC at the end.
Windows keeps time in units of 100 nanoseconds, so the last two digits of
the fraction are always
.BR 00 .
.PP
With
.BR \-f ,
the lines are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B File:
shows the name as given, in double quotes.
.IP \(bu 2
.B ID:
shows the file system ID in hex,
.B Namelen:
the maximum length of a file name, and
.B Type:
the name of the type of file system.
.IP \(bu 2
.B "Block size:"
shows the block size.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Blocks:
shows the
.BR Total: ,
.BR Free: ,
and
.B Available:
numbers of blocks.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Inodes:
shows the
.B Total:
and
.B Free:
numbers of inodes, which are always 0.
.SS Terse output
.B \-t
prints one line of fields separated by single spaces.
For a file it is the same as
.B \-c
with the format
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
%n %s %b %f %u %g %D %i %h %t %T %X %Y %Z %o
.EE
.RE
.PP
and with
.B \-f
the format
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
%n %i %l %t %s %b %f %a %c %d
.EE
.RE
.SS Format directives
In a
.B \-c
format, a
.B %
starts a directive, which is written as one letter.
Between the
.B %
and the letter may come flags, a width, and a precision, as in the C
function
.BR printf :
any of the characters
.BR # ,
.BR \- ,
.BR + ,
.BR . ,
a space, and digits.
An
.B I
is accepted there too, but makes the directive print wrongly.
Numbers are printed as integers and everything else as strings, so
.B %\-10s
pads the size on the right,
.B %08s
pads it with zeros, and
.B %04a
prints
.B 0664
for the permissions
.BR 664 .
.PP
A letter that is not a directive is printed as itself, padded to the
width if there is one, so
.B %q
prints
.BR q .
That includes a letter that is a directive only in the other mode,
such as
.B %N
with
.BR \-f .
.PP
Directives for a file:
.TP
.B %a
The permission bits in octal, such as
.BR 664 .
.TP
.B %A
The file type and permissions as a string of 10 characters, such as
.BR \-rw\-rw\-r\-\- .
The first is
.B \-
for a regular file,
.B d
for a directory,
.B l
for a symbolic link, or
.B c
for a device.
.TP
.B %b
The number of blocks allocated, each of
.B %B
bytes.
.TP
.B %B
The size in bytes of each block that
.B %b
counts.
It is always 512.
.TP
.B %d
The device number in decimal.
It is the serial number of the volume that holds the file, or 0 for a
device.
.TP
.B %D
The device number in hex.
.TP
.B %f
The raw mode in hex: the file type and permission bits, such as
.BR 81b4 .
.TP
.B %F
The file type:
.BR "regular file" ,
.BR "regular empty file" ,
.BR directory ,
.BR "symbolic link" ,
or
.BR "character special file" .
.TP
.B %g
The group ID.
It is always the same as
.BR %u .
.TP
.B %G
The group name.
It is always the same as
.BR %U .
.TP
.B %h
The number of hard links.
.TP
.B %i
The inode number.
.TP
.B %n
The file name, as given.
.TP
.B %N
The file name, as given.
For a symbolic link it is instead the name and the target, each in
single quotes and separated by an arrow, as in
.BR "\(aqzlib\(aq \-> \(aqC:/src/zlib\-1.3.1\(aq" .
The target is written with forward slashes.
Any width or precision is then ignored.
.TP
.B %o
The I/O block size.
It is always 4096.
.TP
.B %s
The size in bytes.
.TP
.B %t
The major device number in hex.
It is always 0.
.TP
.B %T
The minor device number in hex.
It is always 0.
.TP
.B %u
The user ID, such as 4095.
.TP
.B %U
The user name, or
.B UNKNOWN
if the user ID has none; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B %x
The time of last access, in the form described under
.BR "Default output" .
.TP
.B %X
The time of last access in seconds since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, as a
32-bit number; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B %y
The time of last modification.
.TP
.B %Y
The time of last modification in seconds since 1970.
.TP
.B %z
The time of last change, which is the creation time.
.TP
.B %Z
The time of last change in seconds since 1970.
.TP
.B %%
A percent sign.
.PP
Directives for a file system, with
.BR \-f :
.TP
.B %a
The number of free blocks available to the current user.
.TP
.B %b
The total number of blocks.
.TP
.B %c
The total number of inodes.
It is always 0.
.TP
.B %d
The number of free inodes.
It is always 0.
.TP
.B %f
The number of free blocks.
.TP
.B %i
The file system ID in hex.
It is the serial number of the volume followed by 8 zero digits.
.TP
.B %l
The maximum length of a file name.
.TP
.B %n
The file name, as given.
.TP
.B %s
The block size.
.TP
.B %S
The fundamental block size.
It is the same as
.BR %s .
.TP
.B %t
The type of file system in hex.
.TP
.B %T
The type of file system as a name.
.TP
.B %%
A percent sign.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-Lt ,
and may follow the operands unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-c%s ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help .
There is no
.BR \-l ,
although the usage message shows one.
.TP
.BI "\-c " format
Print
.I format
for each file instead of the default or terse output.
Directives are described under
.BR "Format directives" .
Every other character is printed as it is, including a backslash, so
.B \en
is not a newline.
A newline follows
.IR format ,
except that when
.I format
ends in a
.B %
with no letter after it, the
.B %
is printed, any flags and digits after it are dropped, and there is no
newline.
If given more than once, the last
.B \-c
applies.
It overrides
.BR \-t .
.TP
.B \-f
Print the status of the file system that holds each
.IR file .
The file must exist, but only its drive counts: a name that starts with a
drive letter and a colon selects that drive, and any other name, such as
a relative path or one starting with a slash, selects the drive of the
current directory.
When the current directory has no drive letter, as on a network share
named by a UNC path, such a name fails.
.TP
.B \-L
Follow symbolic links: describe the target of a link instead of the link.
A link whose target does not exist is an error.
It has no effect with
.BR \-f .
.TP
.B \-t
Print the terse form; see
.BR "Terse output" .
It has no effect with
.BR \-c .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument only, write a usage message on standard output and
exit with status 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS
When it is
.BR 1 ,
Windows shows its critical-error dialogs, such as for a drive with no
disk in it, instead of the operation failing with an error.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when busybox starts, for a caller
such as
.B cmd.exe
that does not expand them.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so commands run from the kit's shell get the
shell's expansion only.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
The file mode creation mask, in octal.
The default is
.BR 002 .
Its write bits for group and other remove those permissions from the
modes that
.B stat
reports, and its other bits are ignored; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
If set, the first operand ends the options.
.TP
.B TZ
The time zone for the local times of
.BR %x ,
.BR %y ,
and
.BR %z ,
in the form the C library understands, such as
.B UTC0
or
.BR EST5EDT .
Without it, the time zone of Windows applies.
.PP
Output written to a console also depends on
.BR BB_SKIP_ANSI_EMULATION ,
.BR BB_TERMINAL_MODE ,
.BR LANG ,
.BR LC_ALL ,
and
.BR LC_CTYPE ,
as for every busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every
.I file
was examined, or
.B \-\-help
was given.
.TP
.B 1
A
.I file
could not be examined, or the command line was invalid: no
.IR file ,
an unknown option, or a missing argument.
An invalid command line gets a usage message on standard error.
.PP
A file that cannot be examined is reported on standard error as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
stat: can\(aqt stat \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
or with
.BR \-f ,
as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
stat: can\(aqt read file system information for \(aq\fIfile\fR\(aq: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and the remaining files are still processed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the size and modification time of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stat \-c \(aq%s %y\(aq hello.c
29 2026\-10\-19 21:20:34.592248200 \-0400
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the time in UTC:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ TZ=UTC0 stat \-c %y hello.c
2026\-10\-20 01:20:34.592248200 +0000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the permissions and name of several files:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stat \-c \(aq%a %A %n\(aq hello.c hello.exe
664 \-rw\-rw\-r\-\- hello.c
775 \-rwxrwxr\-x hello.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the first fields of the terse form:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stat \-t hello.c | cut \-d\(aq \(aq \-f1\-4
hello.c 29 8 81b4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Tell a junction from what it points to:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stat \-c %F zlib
symbolic link
$ stat \-L \-c %F zlib
directory
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show where a junction points:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stat \-c %N zlib
\(aqzlib\(aq \-> \(aqC:/src/zlib\-1.3.1\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the owner of a system file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stat \-c \(aq%u %U %n\(aq c:/Windows/notepad.exe
0 root c:/Windows/notepad.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Show the bytes available on a drive:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ echo $(( $(stat \-f \-c \(aq%a * %S\(aq c:/) ))
153973358592
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Modes are made up from file attributes, not from the access control
list.
Every file is readable, and writable unless it has the read-only
attribute.
A file is executable if its name ends in
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.BR .cmd ,
in any case.
Otherwise, unless its name ends in
.BR .dll ,
.B stat
reads its first bytes, and the file is executable if it begins with
.B #!
or is a PE program that is not a DLL.
This makes
.B stat
slower on many files, though it tries to leave the access time unchanged.
A directory is readable, writable, and searchable.
The three triples of a mode are the same, except that the write
permission of group and of other is removed when the file mode creation
mask has its bit.
The default mask is
.BR 002 ,
so other cannot write; see
.BR BB_UMASK .
For a file owned by another account, the permissions of other are raised
to those of the owner when the file's access control list gives the
current user read, write, and execute access, except in an elevated
process.
.PP
There are no groups: the group of a file is its owner.
A file owned by the current user has the ID 4095 and the user's name,
with spaces replaced by underscores.
The owner of another local or domain account has the number that ends
its security identifier, when that is from 500 to 4094, and the name
.BR UNKNOWN .
Any other owner, such as Administrators, SYSTEM, or TrustedInstaller, is
.B root
or 0.
When
.B stat
cannot open a file to read its owner, the owner is root and other has no
permissions.
.PP
A Unix device name that busybox accepts, such as
.IR /dev/null ,
is a character device with the numbers 0, 0, the mode 0666, no size, the
current user as its owner, and all three times at the Unix epoch; see
.BR busybox (1).
Its device number, inode number, and blocks are 0, and it has one link.
The only kinds of file are regular files, directories, symbolic links,
and these character devices: there are no block devices, fifos, or
sockets.
.PP
A symbolic link has the mode 0777.
Its size is the length of its target as
.B %N
writes it, and its times are its own.
The target has forward slashes, and for an app execution alias it is the
path of the executable.
.PP
The change time is the time the file was created.
The seconds since the epoch of
.BR %X ,
.BR %Y ,
and
.BR %Z ,
which the terse form also prints, are truncated to 32-bit signed
numbers, so a time after 2038-01-19 03:14:07 UTC prints wrong, usually as
a negative number.
The times in text are not affected, but a time before 1970 or after the
year 3000 prints in text as the Unix epoch, and the fraction of a time
before 1970 prints wrong.
.PP
Allocated sizes are space on disk.
.B stat
assumes a cluster size of 4096 bytes and rounds every nonempty file up to
a multiple of that, which makes
.B %b
a multiple of 8.
A compressed or sparse NTFS file counts the space it occupies, which can
be much less than its length.
A symbolic link counts as 4096 bytes.
A directory counts the size Windows reports for it, rounded up in the
same way, which is 0 for a small NTFS directory.
.PP
The device number is the serial number of the volume, and the inode
number is the 64-bit file index that Windows gives the file.
The number of links of a file is its number of hard links.
That of a directory is 2 plus its number of subdirectories, not counting
symbolic links and junctions, which
.B stat
finds by reading the directory.
A symbolic link to a directory, such as a junction, has the number of
links of that directory.
A file that
.B stat
cannot open, or that has the recall-on-data-access attribute, such as a
cloud placeholder, has device 0, inode 0, and one link, or two for a
directory.
A file with that attribute is owned by the current user, and
.B stat
does not read it to find whether it is executable.
.PP
With
.BR \-f ,
only the drive of the file matters.
.B stat
assumes a block size of 4096 bytes for a volume under 16 TiB, and 8192,
16384, 32768, and 65536 for volumes of at least 16, 32, 64, and 128 TiB.
Free blocks are the free space of the volume and available blocks
the free space for the current user, which a disk quota can reduce.
The type of the file system comes from the name that Windows gives it:
.TP
.B NTFS
The type 5346544e, named
.BR ntfs .
.TP
.BR FAT " and " FAT32
The type 4006, named
.BR fat .
.TP
.B CDFS
The type 9660, named
.BR isofs .
.TP
.B UDF
The type 15013346, named
.BR udf .
.TP
Any other
The type 0, named
.BR UNKNOWN .
This includes exFAT and ReFS.
.PP
Names may use
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
and are printed as given.
A drive-relative path such as
.B c:dir
works.
A file whose full path has 260 or more characters is reported as not
found, even when named by a shorter relative path.
Names may contain any Unicode character on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
Output lines end with LF, not CRLF.
.PP
Differences from GNU
.BR stat :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-t
exist.
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
no
.BR \-Z ,
and no
.BR \-\-printf ,
which would interpret backslash escapes.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no directives for the birth time or the mount point, so
.BR %w ,
.BR %W ,
and
.B %m
print as themselves, and no
.B Birth:
line.
.IP \(bu 2
The name in the
.B File:
line is quoted only for a symbolic link, or with
.BR \-f .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR df (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR jn (1),
.BR ln (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR lsattr (1),
.BR readlink (1),
.BR touch (1)
